<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<!-- ================================================================= -->
<!--  Chapter 8: Handling Missing Values                               -->
<!--                                                                    -->
<!--  Opens with a real, verified story: Abraham Wald and the           -->
<!--  Statistical Research Group's WWII aircraft-survivability work.    -->
<!--  Chosen deliberately over a "disaster" story (unlike ch2/ch4):     -->
<!--  it is the classic case of reasoning correctly, and it is exactly  -->
<!--  about the imputation/deletion-adjacent question this chapter      -->
<!--  needs (why is data missing, and what does that imply about how    -->
<!--  to treat it), which none of the ch4 stories (inconsistency,       -->
<!--  duplication, silent recoding) actually covered.                  -->
<!--  Verified via multiple independent searches before writing:       -->
<!--    - Wald, A. (1943). "A Method of Estimating Plane Vulnerability  -->
<!--      Based on Damage of Survivors." Statistical Research Group,    -->
<!--      Columbia University, memorandum for the National Defense     -->
<!--      Research Committee (reprinted, Center for Naval Analyses,    -->
<!--      1980).                                                       -->
<!--    - Mangel, M. and Samaniego, F.J. (1984). "Abraham Wald's Work   -->
<!--      on Aircraft Survivability." Journal of the American           -->
<!--      Statistical Association, 79(386), 259-267. Peer-reviewed      -->
<!--      formalisation of Wald's original (classified at the time)     -->
<!--      memoranda - used as the authoritative secondary source for    -->
<!--      what his method actually did technically, specifically to    -->
<!--      avoid the oversimplified pop-science "just armour the         -->
<!--      engines" version. His actual method: assume hits are roughly  -->
<!--      uniformly distributed over the airframe by surface area, then -->
<!--      use returning-plane damage counts to infer conditional        -->
<!--      survival/vulnerability by section, explicitly correcting for  -->
<!--      the missing (non-returning) planes rather than reading        -->
<!--      returning-plane damage at face value.                        -->
<!--  MCAR/MAR/MNAR formal definitions and the na.rm/complete.cases     -->
<!--  material are adapted from the old, unbuilt drafts                -->
<!--  (ch6_descriptive_statistics.ptx sec-missing-data, and             -->
<!--  ch6_description_2.ptx subsec-missing-formal), rewritten to open   -->
<!--  with Wald as the MNAR example rather than presenting the taxonomy -->
<!--  cold.                                                             -->
<!-- ================================================================= -->

<chapter xml:id="ch-handling-missing-values" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Handling Missing Values</title>

  <introduction xml:id="intro-handling-missing-values">
    <p>
      <xref ref="ch-exploring-data" /> hit <c>NA</c> the moment it tried to
      compute a mean, and set the problem aside. <xref ref="ch-cleaning-recoding" />
      audited the gaps and started reasoning informally about why
      <c>bill_depth_mm</c>, <c>body_mass_g</c> and <c>sex</c> specifically
      had them. Both chapters promised a proper answer later. This is later.
    </p>

    <p>
      The central idea of this chapter is easy to state and easy to
      underestimate: <em>why</em> a value is missing determines what you are
      allowed to do about it. Sometimes missingness is background noise,
      safely ignorable. Sometimes it is the single most important piece of
      information in the dataset. The story below is the case where getting
      that distinction right won a real argument, in wartime, against
      everyone's first instinct.
    </p>
  </introduction>

<!-- ================================================================= -->
<!--  SECTION 1: Wald and the bombers                                  -->
<!-- ================================================================= -->

  <section xml:id="sec-wald-survivorship">
    <title>The Bombers That Didn't Come Back</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        During the Second World War, the US military examined bombers
        returning from missions over Germany and recorded where each one
        had been hit. The damage was not evenly spread. It clustered
        heavily on the wings and fuselage, far more than on the engines.
        The obvious recommendation followed immediately: reinforce the
        wings and fuselage, since that is clearly where the bullets go.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-wald-the-catch">
      <title>The data was correct. The dataset was incomplete.</title>

      <p>
        The Statistical Research Group (SRG) at Columbia University, a
        team the US military had assembled to bring rigorous statistics to
        exactly these kinds of operational problems, included the
        mathematician Abraham Wald. Wald's objection to the obvious
        recommendation was not that the damage counts were wrong. They were
        almost certainly accurate. His objection was about which planes had
        been counted at all.
      </p>

      <p>
        Every bomber in that dataset had survived its mission and made it
        home. A bomber hit badly enough in the engine did not return to be
        inspected, photographed, and added to the tally. The data was not a
        random sample of <q>where bombers get hit.</q> It was a sample of
        <q>where bombers can be hit and still make it back</q> <mdash /> and
        those are very different questions. Heavy wing and fuselage damage
        was common in the surviving planes precisely <em>because</em> a
        plane could absorb a great deal of damage there and keep flying.
        The scarcity of engine damage in the data did not mean the engines
        were rarely hit. It meant a hit there was rarely survived, which is
        why those planes were missing from the dataset entirely.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-wald-the-method">
      <title>What Wald actually did about it</title>

      <p>
        Wald did not simply assert the intuition and stop there. Across a
        series of memoranda for the SRG, later formalised and republished by
        Mangel and Samaniego (1984), he built a statistical method for
        estimating vulnerability by aircraft section that explicitly
        accounted for the missing planes rather than reading the returning
        planes' damage at face value. Roughly: assuming hits were
        distributed over the airframe more or less in proportion to each
        section's surface area, the pattern of damage on <em>survivors</em>
        could be used to infer how survivable a hit to each section actually
        was <mdash /> and a section that rarely showed damage on returning
        planes, despite having no reason to be hit less often, was exactly
        the section a hit was least likely to be survived.
      </p>

      <p>
        The recommendation that followed was the reverse of the obvious one:
        reinforce the sections that were <em>under</em>-represented in the
        damage data, not over-represented, because that absence was the
        signal.
      </p>

      <insight xml:id="insight-wald-lesson">
        <title>The missing data was not noise. It was the finding.</title>
        <p>
          It would have been easy to treat the absent engine damage as
          nothing more than a smaller sample size for that section, safely
          ignorable, and analyse the rest of the data as if the missing
          rows simply were not there. Wald's insight was recognising that
          the missingness itself was informative: which planes were absent
          from the dataset depended directly on the thing being measured
          (survivability), not on some unrelated cause. Deleting the
          incomplete cases and moving on would not have lost a little
          statistical power. It would have inverted the conclusion.
        </p>
      </insight>

      <p>
        <term>Sources:</term> Wald, A. (1943). <em>A Method of Estimating
        Plane Vulnerability Based on Damage of Survivors.</em> Statistical
        Research Group, Columbia University, memorandum for the National
        Defense Research Committee (reprinted, Center for Naval Analyses,
        1980). Mangel, M. and Samaniego, F.J. (1984). <q>Abraham Wald's Work
        on Aircraft Survivability.</q> <em>Journal of the American
        Statistical Association</em>, 79(386), 259<ndash />267.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>

<!-- ================================================================= -->
<!--  SECTION 2: naming the pattern                                    -->
<!-- ================================================================= -->

  <section xml:id="sec-missing-taxonomy">
    <title>Naming Why Data Is Missing</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        Wald's case is the extreme, high-stakes version of a question every
        missing value forces you to ask: <em>does the fact that this value
        is missing depend on the value itself?</em> Statistics has three
        formal answers, and which one applies changes what you are allowed
        to do next.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-mcar-mar-mnar">
      <title>MCAR, MAR, and MNAR</title>

      <p>
        Let <m>R_i</m> be an indicator: <m>R_i = 1</m> if observation
        <m>x_i</m> was recorded, <m>R_i = 0</m> if it is missing.
      </p>

      <definition>
        <term>Missing Completely at Random (MCAR)</term>
        <statement>
          <p>
            The probability of a value being missing is unrelated to
            anything, observed or not: <m>P(R_i = 0 \mid x_i) = P(R_i = 0)</m>.
            A clipboard dropped in the mud, destroying a random page of
            field notes, is close to MCAR.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </definition>

      <definition>
        <term>Missing at Random (MAR)</term>
        <statement>
          <p>
            The probability of a value being missing may depend on other,
            <em>observed</em> variables, but not on the missing value itself
            once those are accounted for. If bill depth is more likely to be
            missing for one species, but entirely because that species'
            measurement protocol changed partway through the season
            <mdash /> a fact recorded elsewhere in the data <mdash /> that
            is MAR.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </definition>

      <definition>
        <term>Missing Not at Random (MNAR)</term>
        <statement>
          <p>
            The probability of a value being missing depends on the
            <em>unobserved value itself</em>, even after accounting for
            everything else you observed. Wald's bombers are the canonical
            case: whether a damage report exists for a given engine hit
            depends entirely on how severe that hit was <mdash /> the exact
            quantity being studied.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </definition>

      <table xml:id="table-missingness-implications">
        <title>What each pattern implies for a fix</title>
        <tabular halign="left">
          <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
            <cell>Pattern</cell>
            <cell>Deleting incomplete rows</cell>
            <cell>What's needed instead</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>MCAR</cell>
            <cell>Safe: costs statistical power, introduces no bias</cell>
            <cell>Usually nothing beyond reporting the reduced <m>n</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>MAR</cell>
            <cell>Biased if it ignores the related observed variable</cell>
            <cell>Model or impute using the variables missingness depends on</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>MNAR</cell>
            <cell>Biased, potentially severely, in a direction you can't measure from the remaining data alone</cell>
            <cell>External information, a model of the missingness process itself, or explicit sensitivity analysis</cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </table>

      <p>
        Note the asymmetry: MCAR and MAR can, in principle, be fixed using
        only the data you have. MNAR cannot. If the reason a value is
        missing is the value itself, nothing in the observed data can fully
        recover what was lost <mdash /> which is exactly why Wald needed an
        external assumption (roughly uniform exposure across the airframe)
        rather than a formula applied to the survivors' data alone.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>

<!-- ================================================================= -->
<!--  SECTION 3: applied to the flamingo data                          -->
<!-- ================================================================= -->

  <section xml:id="sec-missing-applied">
    <title>Handling Missing Values, Applied</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        Return to the gaps <xref ref="ch-cleaning-recoding" /> found and
        reasoned about informally: 4 missing bill depths, 8 missing masses,
        7 missing sexes.
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        colSums(is.na(flamingos))

        #&gt;           lake        species bill_length_mm  bill_depth_mm  leg_length_mm
        #&gt;              0              0              0              4              0
        #&gt;    body_mass_g            sex           year
        #&gt;              8              7              0
      </program>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-flamingo-mechanism">
      <title>Which pattern applies to which gap</title>

      <p>
        <xref ref="ch-cleaning-recoding" /> already sketched plausible
        mechanisms; now they can be named properly.
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            A scale battery failing mid-morning, so every mass after that
            point goes unrecorded regardless of how heavy the bird was:
            this is <term>MCAR</term>-ish, provided nothing about which
            birds happened to be weighed that morning correlates with their
            mass.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Determining sex requiring closer handling, which the field team
            reserves for calmer birds: if <q>calm enough to handle
            closely</q> is itself unrelated to a bird's other recorded
            traits, this could be MCAR; if calmer birds are systematically a
            particular species or size class, which the dataset also
            records, it becomes <term>MAR</term> instead.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            If, on the other hand, the heaviest and most powerful birds are
            specifically the ones that struggle free before a mass reading
            can be taken, that is <term>MNAR</term>: the missingness of a
            mass value depends on the mass itself, the exact structure of
            Wald's problem, and it means the 336 recorded masses are not a
            fair sample of all 344 birds' true weights.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        The dataset alone cannot tell you which of these is true. That has
        to come from knowing how the data was collected <mdash /> the same
        conclusion <xref ref="ch-cleaning-recoding" /> reached, now with the
        vocabulary to say precisely what is at stake in each case.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-na-rm-precise">
      <title>What <c>na.rm = TRUE</c> actually does</title>

      <p>
        It removes the missing observations and computes on what remains.
        Stated plainly: <em>you are no longer analysing the sample you
        thought you were.</em>
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        mean(flamingos$body_mass_g, na.rm = TRUE)
        #&gt; [1] 1957.405
      </program>

      <p>
        This is the mean of 336 birds, not 344, and it is essential to be
        clear that <c>na.rm = TRUE</c> is <em>not</em> imputation. It does
        not estimate the missing values. It excludes those birds from the
        calculation entirely, silently, unless you check <c>sum(!is.na(...))</c>
        yourself. If the missingness is MCAR, that exclusion costs you
        precision and nothing else. If it is MNAR, as with mass, it may
        cost you accuracy in a direction you cannot detect from the
        remaining 336 rows alone.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-complete-cases-applied">
      <title>Complete cases and listwise deletion</title>

      <program language="r">
        sum(complete.cases(flamingos))
        #&gt; [1] 325

        nrow(flamingos)
        #&gt; [1] 344
      </program>

      <p>
        Only 325 of 344 rows are complete: 19 rows, 5.5%, have at least one
        missing value somewhere, more than the 8 missing masses alone,
        because the gaps fall in different rows for different variables.
        Dropping every incomplete row (<term>listwise
        deletion</term><idx><h>listwise deletion</h></idx>) is convenient
        and keeps every analysis on an identical sample, but it discards
        data unnecessarily: a bird missing only <c>sex</c> still has a
        perfectly good mass measurement. For descriptive work, prefer
        <c>na.rm = TRUE</c> variable by variable over blanket listwise
        deletion, and report the <m>n</m> for each.
      </p>

      <remark xml:id="remark-report-n-ch8">
        <title>Report the n</title>
        <p>
          Never report a mean, a standard deviation, or any other summary
          without also reporting the <m>n</m> it was computed from. A
          summary over 344 birds, 336, or 325 are different claims about
          different samples, and a table that omits <m>n</m> makes them
          indistinguishable.
        </p>
      </remark>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-imputation-caution">
      <title>Imputation is a claim, not a fix</title>

      <p>
        A common third option, beyond na.rm-style exclusion and full
        listwise deletion, is <term>imputation</term><idx><h>imputation</h></idx>:
        filling each missing value with an estimate, often the variable's
        mean or a model-based prediction, so no rows need to be dropped at
        all.
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        mass_imputed &lt;- ifelse(
          is.na(flamingos$body_mass_g),
          mean(flamingos$body_mass_g, na.rm = TRUE),
          flamingos$body_mass_g
        )
      </program>

      <p>
        This line runs without complaint and produces a column with no
        missing values at all. It is also, if the missingness is MNAR,
        actively worse than doing nothing: it does not just fail to fix the
        bias, it hides it, replacing 8 honestly-missing values with 8
        confident-looking numbers built from the very mean that bias has
        already distorted. Imputation is a reasonable tool under MCAR or
        MAR, where the observed data genuinely contains the information
        needed to make a defensible estimate. Under MNAR it requires an
        external model of <em>why</em> the data is missing <mdash /> Wald's
        uniform-exposure assumption is exactly that kind of model <mdash />
        not a one-line formula applied to whatever happens to remain.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>

<!-- ================================================================= -->
<!--  OBJECTIVES                                                       -->
<!-- ================================================================= -->

  <objectives xml:id="objectives-handling-missing-values">
    <title>By the end of this topic you will be able to</title>
    <ul>
      <li><p>Explain why the reason a value is missing determines what you may safely do about it.</p></li>
      <li><p>Distinguish MCAR, MAR, and MNAR, and identify which applies to a given case.</p></li>
      <li><p>Explain what <c>na.rm = TRUE</c> and <c>complete.cases()</c> actually do, and what they do not do.</p></li>
      <li><p>Recognise when imputation is a reasonable tool and when it disguises rather than fixes a bias.</p></li>
    </ul>
  </objectives>

<!-- ================================================================= -->
<!--  EXERCISES                                                        -->
<!-- ================================================================= -->

  <exercises xml:id="exercises-handling-missing-values">
    <title>Exercises</title>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-missing-wald-recap">
      <statement>
        <p>
          In your own words, explain why deleting the aircraft with the
          heaviest hypothetical engine damage <mdash /> if the SRG had
          somehow had partial records for a few of them <mdash /> would
          still not have solved Wald's problem. What, specifically, does
          MNAR data require that listwise deletion cannot provide?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-missing-classify-flamingo">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <xref ref="ss-flamingo-mechanism" /> gives three different
          possible mechanisms for the missing <c>body_mass_g</c> values,
          corresponding to MCAR, MAR, and MNAR. For each mechanism, describe
          one piece of additional information, beyond what
          <c>kenya_flamingos.csv</c> already contains, that would let you
          tell which mechanism actually applies.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-missing-imputation-risk">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Using the code in <xref ref="ss-imputation-caution" /> as a
          starting point, explain what would happen to the mean and the
          standard deviation of <c>body_mass_g</c> if you used
          <c>mass_imputed</c> instead of the original column with
          <c>na.rm = TRUE</c>. Which one changes, which one doesn't, and
          why does that matter for anyone reading a report built on this
          column?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-missing-sex-mechanism">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Propose a plausible MNAR mechanism for the 7 missing
          <c>sex</c> values, distinct from the one given in
          <xref ref="ss-flamingo-mechanism" />. Then propose a plausible
          MCAR mechanism for the same variable. What would you need to know
          about how the data was collected to decide between them?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
  </exercises>

</chapter>
